<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wainganga River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wainganga is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, about 580 km long, rising in the Seoni district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> and flowing south through eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> to join the Wardha, forming the Pranhita, a tributary of the Godavari. Its fertile valley was the core of Gond kingdoms such as Chanda and later part of the Bhonsle kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wainganga-river.jpg" alt="Wainganga River" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Sushant852 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Bridge_on_Wainganga_River_in_Bhandara_City-_2014-06-18_13-50.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
